1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,200
MyCampusView Academy. Robotics 101, video 13: Troubleshooting like an engineer.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,425
Real engineers spend a lot of time fixing things.

3
00:00:09,475 --> 00:00:10,856
That is normal.

4
00:00:10,906 --> 00:00:15,150
Here is how to troubleshoot like an engineer.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,711
First observe carefully: what exactly happens?

6
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:22,639
Then make a guess about the cause.

7
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:28,150
Change only one thing, test again, and write down what happened.

8
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:34,110
If the robot does nothing at all, check the battery and the

9
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:40,470
switch, check that every ground is connected, and make sure the upload

10
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,650
finished without errors.

11
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:49,671
If the robot spins in circles, one motor is probably wired backwards.

12
00:00:49,721 --> 00:00:56,650
Swap that motor's two wires and test each wheel with the motor test sketch.

13
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:03,684
If the robot turns away from the line, the left and right

14
00:01:03,734 --> 00:01:06,958
sensors or rules are swapped.

15
00:01:07,008 --> 00:01:12,715
Swap the sensor wires on D2 and D3, or swap the two

16
00:01:12,765 --> 00:01:15,650
turning rules in the code.

17
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:22,642
If the robot stops on a plain curve, both sensors may think

18
00:01:22,692 --> 00:01:24,267
they see black.

19
00:01:24,317 --> 00:01:26,758
They are too sensitive.

20
00:01:26,808 --> 00:01:33,150
Turn the knobs until white reads as white, then test again.

21
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:37,206
The Serial Monitor is your best friend.

22
00:01:37,256 --> 00:01:44,650
If both sensors say BLACK over white paper, you have found the problem: calibration.

23
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:51,500
Next: The final challenge.
